## Decisions

- **This plan is small because the heavy lifting is in the
  prerequisite.** The brain detector (`buildBrainState`) owns the
  vocabulary collapse from five states to four, the rename from
  `connector` to `name`, and the removal of `rootPath`. This tool is
  a verbatim forwarder — once the upstream type changes, the
  forwarder's response shape adapts automatically through the type
  system. The plan exists at all because (a) the test fixtures
  hardcode the old shape and need explicit updates, (b) the
  `name`-forwarding contract needs explicit test coverage at this
  surface, and (c) the spec calls out specific tripwires
  (`rootPath`, `connector`, `invalid-path` re-introduction) that
  warrant explicit `not.toHaveProperty` assertions to keep the
  invariant testable.
- **`not.toHaveProperty` for retired fields, not just type-system
  absence.** The spec's `outcomes.undesired` explicitly forbids
  re-introducing `rootPath`, `connector`, and `invalid-path` under
  any name. Type-system absence enforces the contract at compile
  time; `not.toHaveProperty` enforces it at runtime so a future
  refactor that loosens a type (e.g. adds a passthrough field) trips
  the test immediately. The redundancy is deliberate — the cost of
  one extra assertion per test is small; the cost of a regression
  going undetected is large.
- **Fixtures live inline in the test file, not in a shared utility.**
  The buildBrainState plan suggested extracting fixtures to
  `src/test/` if both files would benefit. This plan keeps fixtures
  inline because (a) the four `BrainState` shapes are small and
  trivial to declare, (b) no other test file imports them, (c) the
  existing pattern in this file is inline declaration. Extract only
  when the duplication actually exists across files; pre-extracting
  for hypothetical reuse is dead-on-arrival abstraction.
- **`name: "obsidian"` as the canonical fixture value.** Mirrors the
  spec's example response objects (`.aide` lines 209-273) so anyone
  reading the tests sees the same brand label the spec showcases. The
  package never branches on this value, so the choice is purely
  cosmetic — using the spec's value keeps tests, spec, and
  documentation in lockstep. A second test that uses a different
  `name` (e.g. `"notion"`) is unnecessary at this layer because
  detection-uniformity-across-names is a property of the detector
  (verified in `buildBrainState/index.test.ts`), not this passive
  forwarder.
- **The orchestrator file changes are minimal-or-none.** The
  existing `info()` function already forwards `buildBrainState`'s
  output unchanged into `result.brain`. Step 1 verifies this stays
  true under the new contract; the only foreseeable edits are JSDoc
  prose if it enumerates state names that no longer exist. No
  pipeline reshaping, no helper extraction, no new imports.
